The `log.warn` call concatenates three separate template literals with `+`. Per the project's code-style convention, this should be a single template literal to avoid unnecessary string concatenation:
```js
log.warn(
  `The tick interval "${every}${interval}" would generate ${estimatedTicks} ticks, which exceeds the maximum allowed (${MAX_TICK_COUNT}). This may indicate an invalid date or time range. Skipping custom tick interval.`
);
```

`dateFormat.trim()` is evaluated twice in the newly added `isTimestampFormat` check (line 307), and is also called in each of the two `log.debug` statements directly above it (lines 301–302), giving a total of four `.trim()` calls in this `else` branch. Cache the trimmed value at the top of the `else` block:
```js
const trimmedFormat = dateFormat.trim();
// then use trimmedFormat everywhere below
```

Calling `expect(err.message).to.include('Invalid date')` inside the `uncaught:exception` handler is fragile. If any _other_ uncaught exception fires during the test (e.g., a network error, a D3 rendering error), the `expect` call will throw inside the handler, producing an opaque Chai assertion failure rather than a clear test failure. A more defensive pattern only captures the expected error type:
```js
cy.on('uncaught:exception', (err) => {
  if (err.message.includes('Invalid date')) {
    errorCaught = true;
  }
  return false; // prevent Cypress from failing on any uncaught exception
});
```
The assertion on `errorCaught` at the end of the test is then sufficient to verify the expected error occurred.
